# Dependencies
node_modules/
# also match a node_modules symlink specifically (a symlink is not a
# directory, so the trailing-slash pattern above won't catch it - this
# regressed onto main once already via `git add -A` over a dev symlink)
**/node_modules

# Build output
dist/
.astro/

# Environment
#
# A PATTERN, NOT A LIST. These three were named individually, so `api/.env.bak` - a backup
# somebody made beside a real one - was never ignored and was committed on 31 July 2026 with a
# live JWT signing secret in it. The repository is public.
#
# Every name that starts with `.env` now, and every directory, so a copy made in `api/` or
# `client/` is caught the same way as one at the root. `.env.example` is deliberately excluded
# below, because that one is meant to be tracked.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# ...except the template, which carries no values and exists to be read.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Prisma
api/dist/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/
*.sw?

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Live journey-test harness output. Never committed: these are raw transcripts of
# every check-in in a run - 122,000 lines from one eighteen-ground pass - and on a
# real deployment they would be people's own accounts of their work. The harness
# that produces them IS committed; what it produces is not. The written
# assessment is the deliverable, and it is kept.
api/journey/out/
api/journey/out-cohort/
api/journey/org-sim/out/*.json
api/journey/org-sim/out-paid/
api/journey/org-sim/out-verify/

# TypeScript incremental build cache - a local artifact, not source.
# It was tracked, so every typecheck showed up as a file change in every diff.
*.tsbuildinfo
.vercel

# Journey drivers written to run a whole setup end to end. Scratch, not product.
api/journey/

# EXCEPT THE TWO THAT DEFINE DONE, which are not scratch and were one `rm` from gone.
#
# `the-real-gate` drives a lead's actual path - entry chat, a workplan into Context, targets, three
# rounds, the close - and prints what a person reads at each stage. It is the acceptance standard:
# "the conversation has to be worthwhile and make sense from start to close, the reports have to
# make sense, and the lead has to feel they are tracking what they need accurately." It found five
# defects that 1,715 green structural checks could not see.
#
# `two-scenarios` is the regression net underneath it: every situation, every surface, after every
# round.
#
# The last thing this repo lost to an ignore rule was the report templates - nine surfaces had been
# built against a file nobody could open any more, and rebuilding it from memory took a night. A
# thing the team's standard depends on does not live outside the repo.
!api/journey/the-real-gate/
!api/journey/two-scenarios/

# Audit and fix-plan working notes. Written while reading the product, kept on disk, never in a PR:
# they are how the work was decided, not the work. The product docs (api-terms, edge-protection)
# stay tracked - those are things the product itself points at.
docs/audit/
